[Music]  after nearly two weeks of competition  for more than four thousand of the  world's top disabled athletes the  paralympic games come to a close  tomorrow this morning lucy craft  introduces us to one of one u.s athlete  who's a star both inside and far beyond  the sports stadium  in her paralympic debut blind swimmer  anastasia pogonis got right down to  business grabbing team usa's first gold  medal with a world record time in the  400 meter freestyle an assistant gave  her the news with a tap on the head when  i came into the wall i got one tap for  gold two taps for silver and three taps  for bronze and i got that one tap and i  will never forget that moment  so you knew before anybody had said  anything to you that you had won the  race  yeah because they're not allowed to talk  to me on the playing field um  so i got that one tap and i knew that i  had one gold  the 17 year old later won bronze in the  200 meter individual medley hagonis  competes in the s11 class the most  severely visually impaired  an autoimmune disease left her without  sight and despondent just three years  ago my parents would drive me out of the  house just to take me for a five minute  walk while i was literally kicking and  screaming and throwing a tantrum for  going on a five minute walk  but now she's a champion swimmer and a  tick-tock star with two million  followers mixing goofy humor with brutal  honesty pagonus demystifies life as a  fun-loving fashion-forward teen who just  happens to be blind they assume that i  have to wear blacked-out sunglasses and  have my cane and be going around like  this when no i can wear makeup i can be  an elite athlete i can do all these  things and just showing people that they  need to not have that stereotype  she aims to be back in the water at the  next paralympics in 2024.  cbs this morning saturday lucy craft  tokyo  that is the olympic spirit no doubt

